WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.425
Last time, we kept a binary search tree

2
00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:08.290
balanced by repairing it, with rotations

3
00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:10.260
after each insertion.

4
00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:12.716
Here is a different idea.

5
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.183
Build the tree so that it can never become

6
00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.450
unbalanced in the first place.

7
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.466
The trick is to let a node

8
00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:21.160
hold more than one key.

9
00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.100
This is the two-four tree.

10
00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.472
We'll see what a node can hold

11
00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:28.500
and how search works

12
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.020
how an overfull node splits

13
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.700
how an empty node borrows or merges

14
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.410
and why the height is always logarithmic.

15
00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:39.771
A node with two keys has three children.

16
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.938
The first child holds keys smaller

17
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.060
than the first key

18
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:45.240
the second holds keys between the two

19
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.470
and the third holds the rest.

20
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.070
In general

21
00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:53.030
a node with k keys has k plus one children.

22
00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:54.513
Searching is still simple.

23
00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.023
Compare against the keys in the

24
00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:57.450
node from left to right

25
00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.580
and follow the child where your key falls.

26
00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.340
A two-four tree has two rules.

27
00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:06.380
Every node has two, three, or four children

28
00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.960
which means one, two, or three keys.

29
00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.850
And all the leaves are at the same depth.

30
00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:15.220
To insert a key, search down to a leaf

31
00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:18.390
and put the key into that leaf, in order.

32
00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.030
A leaf can hold three keys.

33
00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:24.020
Insert a fourth, and it is overfull.

34
00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:26.360
The fix is a split.

35
00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.817
The third key moves up into the parent.

36
00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.955
The first two keys stay together

37
00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.200
as a left node.

38
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.570
The last key becomes a right node.

39
00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.110
Try it.

40
00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:39.090
Insert four, six, twelve.

41
00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.510
The leaf is full, with three keys.

42
00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:43.270
Now insert fifteen.

43
00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:46.550
The leaf splits, twelve moves up

44
00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:48.310
and since there was no parent

45
00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:49.800
twelve becomes a new root.

46
00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:52.630
A split can overflow the parent

47
00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:54.110
which then splits too

48
00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:55.460
and so on up the tree.

49
00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:57.840
If the root itself splits

50
00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.874
we create a new root above it.

51
00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:02.420
That is the only way a two-four

52
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:03.770
tree grows taller.

53
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.330
Insert the numbers one to thirteen

54
00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:07.370
in order.

55
00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:10.490
After twelve keys, the tree has two levels.

56
00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.830
The thirteenth key splits a leaf

57
00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:16.160
which overflows the root, which splits too.

58
00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:18.200
A new root appears

59
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.138
and the tree has three levels.

60
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.409
Every leaf got one level deeper

61
00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:23.990
at the same moment.

62
00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:26.100
Pause and predict.

63
00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.220
A binary search tree grows at the bottom

64
00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.620
a new key becomes a new leaf

65
00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.250
below the old ones.

66
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.030
Where does a two-four tree grow?

67
00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.010
At the bottom, on one side?

68
00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:39.430
At the bottom, everywhere?

69
00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:41.360
Or at the top?

70
00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:43.080
At the top.

71
00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:45.360
Only a root split adds a level

72
00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.360
and it adds one above every leaf at once.

73
00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.180
So all the leaves stay at the same depth

74
00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:52.110
always.

75
00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:58.100
Let's insert eleven keys: four, six, twelve

76
00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:04.000
fifteen, three, five, ten, eight, eleven

77
00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.170
thirteen, eighteen.

78
00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.730
Fifteen splits the first leaf.

79
00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.590
Five splits the left leaf

80
00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:13.621
and five moves up next to twelve.

81
00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:15.315
Ten and eight fit into a leaf

82
00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:17.130
with no split at all.

83
00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:18.550
Eleven splits a leaf

84
00:03:18.550 --> 00:03:21.800
and the root becomes full, with three keys.

85
00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:25.840
In the end, eleven keys sit in two levels

86
00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:27.630
after three splits.

87
00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:30.780
To delete a key from an internal node

88
00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.520
swap it with its in-order successor

89
00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.240
the smallest key to its right.

90
00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.880
That key always sits in a leaf.

91
00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.810
Then delete it from the leaf.

92
00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:44.150
If the leaf still has a key, we are done.

93
00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:47.560
If it becomes empty, it has underflowed.

94
00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:49.880
The first fix is a transfer.

95
00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:52.180
If a sibling has two or more keys

96
00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:54.365
it gives its nearest key to the parent

97
00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:55.768
and the parent gives a key

98
00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:57.390
down to the empty node.

99
00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:01.050
If no sibling can spare a key, we fuse.

100
00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.133
The empty node, a sibling

101
00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:04.683
and the key between them in the

102
00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:07.110
parent merge into one node.

103
00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:09.630
The parent now has one key fewer

104
00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:11.460
so it might underflow too.

105
00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.270
Why is the height logarithmic?

106
00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:16.190
All leaves are at the same depth

107
00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:18.480
so count the keys by levels.

108
00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:22.200
If every node is a two-node, with one key

109
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.260
a tree with h levels holds two to the h

110
00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:26.820
minus one keys.

111
00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:28.700
If every node is a four-node

112
00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:31.680
with three keys, it holds four to the h

113
00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:32.720
minus one.

114
00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.390
Every real tree is somewhere in between.

115
00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:37.606
So with n keys

116
00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:39.629
the number of levels is between

117
00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:42.450
log base four of n plus one

118
00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:45.020
and log base two of n plus one.

119
00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:46.880
For a thousand keys

120
00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.180
that is between five and nine levels.

121
00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:51.500
For a million keys

122
00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:53.170
between ten and nineteen.

123
00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:54.563
In practice

124
00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:56.696
we measured six levels for the numbers

125
00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:59.260
one to a thousand inserted in order

126
00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:01.208
and eleven levels for a

127
00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:02.450
hundred thousand keys

128
00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:04.360
in order or at random.

129
00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:05.822
To sum up.

130
00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:07.704
A two-four tree lets a node

131
00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:09.790
hold one to three keys

132
00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:12.500
and keeps every leaf at the same depth.

133
00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:16.460
An overfull node splits and pushes a key up.

134
00:05:16.460 --> 00:05:19.400
An empty node borrows with a transfer

135
00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:21.810
or merges with a fuse.

136
00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:25.210
The tree grows and shrinks only at the root

137
00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:27.280
so it is always balanced.

138
00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:28.928
In the next video

139
00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:30.784
you'll see that a red-black tree

140
00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:33.220
is a two-four tree in disguise.

141
00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:34.870
First, the code.

142
00:05:35.497 --> 00:05:37.867
Here is the two-four tree in Java.

143
00:05:37.867 --> 00:05:40.787
A node holds two lists: its keys

144
00:05:40.787 --> 00:05:42.297
and its children.

145
00:05:42.877 --> 00:05:46.067
The tree is generic over any comparable key.

146
00:05:46.067 --> 00:05:48.361
A leaf has an empty children list

147
00:05:48.427 --> 00:05:50.358
and an internal node has one

148
00:05:50.427 --> 00:05:52.037
more child than keys.

149
00:05:52.387 --> 00:05:54.557
A small record, Split

150
00:05:54.557 --> 00:05:56.777
carries the answer of a split

151
00:05:56.777 --> 00:05:59.387
the middle key, and the new right node.

152
00:05:59.967 --> 00:06:02.177
Insert checks that the key is new

153
00:06:02.177 --> 00:06:03.477
and recurses.

154
00:06:03.477 --> 00:06:06.877
A non-null Split means the root overflowed

155
00:06:06.877 --> 00:06:09.327
so we build a new root above it.

156
00:06:09.677 --> 00:06:11.564
lowerBound finds where the key

157
00:06:11.627 --> 00:06:13.187
belongs inside a node.

158
00:06:13.187 --> 00:06:16.327
In a leaf we add the key at that index.

159
00:06:16.327 --> 00:06:18.687
In an internal node we recurse

160
00:06:18.687 --> 00:06:20.877
and absorb a split from the child.

161
00:06:21.227 --> 00:06:23.977
If the node now has more than three keys

162
00:06:23.977 --> 00:06:24.937
it splits

163
00:06:24.937 --> 00:06:27.267
and returns the Split to its caller.

164
00:06:27.847 --> 00:06:30.907
The split keeps the first two keys and sends

165
00:06:30.977 --> 00:06:33.345
the last key to a new right node.

166
00:06:33.417 --> 00:06:34.979
The third key is the middle

167
00:06:35.037 --> 00:06:36.207
one that goes up.

168
00:06:36.557 --> 00:06:40.187
Here is a classic Java trap.

169
00:06:40.187 --> 00:06:42.167
subList does not copy.

170
00:06:42.167 --> 00:06:44.427
It returns a view of the same list.

171
00:06:44.427 --> 00:06:48.207
So we wrap the slices in new ArrayList

172
00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:49.637
to cut the connection.

173
00:06:50.217 --> 00:06:52.607
Remove mirrors insert

174
00:06:52.607 --> 00:06:54.847
replace the key with its successor

175
00:06:54.847 --> 00:06:56.407
delete from the leaf

176
00:06:56.407 --> 00:06:59.457
and on the way back call fixUnderflow.

177
00:06:59.807 --> 00:07:02.101
An empty node tries a transfer

178
00:07:02.177 --> 00:07:03.577
from the left sibling

179
00:07:03.577 --> 00:07:05.437
then from the right sibling.

180
00:07:05.437 --> 00:07:08.647
If neither can spare a key, it fuses.

181
00:07:08.997 --> 00:07:10.350
One more trap.

182
00:07:10.447 --> 00:07:12.748
keys.remove with an int index

183
00:07:12.827 --> 00:07:14.447
removes by position.

184
00:07:14.447 --> 00:07:16.327
With an Integer object

185
00:07:16.327 --> 00:07:18.007
it would remove by value.

186
00:07:18.007 --> 00:07:20.677
Our code always passes an int index.

187
00:07:21.257 --> 00:07:22.687
Run the demo.

188
00:07:22.687 --> 00:07:27.307
Splits at fifteen, five, and eleven.

189
00:07:27.307 --> 00:07:30.567
Then removing four is a transfer

190
00:07:30.567 --> 00:07:33.297
and removing eight is a fuse.

191
00:07:33.647 --> 00:07:35.437
The output is identical

192
00:07:35.437 --> 00:07:36.737
character for character

193
00:07:36.737 --> 00:07:38.007
to the Python output.

194
00:07:38.587 --> 00:07:40.462
The main test inserts and

195
00:07:40.537 --> 00:07:41.794
removes random keys

196
00:07:41.857 --> 00:07:43.383
two hundred fifty operations

197
00:07:43.437 --> 00:07:45.537
on each of forty trees

198
00:07:45.537 --> 00:07:47.067
compared with a TreeSet.

199
00:07:47.417 --> 00:07:50.687
checkInvariants runs after every operation.

200
00:07:50.687 --> 00:07:54.091
A second test removes every key, one by one

201
00:07:54.167 --> 00:07:56.016
and checks that the tree stays valid

202
00:07:56.067 --> 00:07:57.697
all the way down to empty.

203
00:07:58.277 --> 00:07:59.924
Common mistakes.

204
00:08:00.027 --> 00:08:01.817
Forgetting to move the children when

205
00:08:01.867 --> 00:08:03.987
splitting an internal node.

206
00:08:03.987 --> 00:08:05.837
Forgetting the root split.

207
00:08:06.187 --> 00:08:09.077
Forgetting that subList is only a view.

208
00:08:09.077 --> 00:08:11.377
And moving a key in a transfer

209
00:08:11.377 --> 00:08:13.547
but not the child that goes with it.

210
00:08:13.897 --> 00:08:16.566
Our checkInvariants throws an AssertionError

211
00:08:16.627 --> 00:08:20.107
when node sizes, child counts, key order

212
00:08:20.107 --> 00:08:21.453
or leaf depth are wrong.

213
00:08:21.507 --> 00:08:23.711
We call it after every random

214
00:08:23.787 --> 00:08:25.337
operation in the tests.

215
00:08:25.917 --> 00:08:27.847
In the standard library

216
00:08:27.847 --> 00:08:30.807
Java has no two-four tree.

217
00:08:30.807 --> 00:08:33.267
TreeMap, which we'll meet in video three

218
00:08:33.267 --> 00:08:34.565
is a red-black tree

219
00:08:34.627 --> 00:08:36.991
and a red-black tree is exactly

220
00:08:37.067 --> 00:08:39.317
a two-four tree in disguise.

221
00:08:39.667 --> 00:08:41.717
Next video: the same tree

222
00:08:41.717 --> 00:08:44.387
drawn with red and black binary nodes.

